The paper is a benchmark of LLMs on table information seeking. RAG is mentioned only in passing ("pseudo-relevant tables, common in retrieval-augmented systems"). It does not propose or evaluate a RAG method. The experiments give LLMs tables directly, with robustness tested against pseudo-relevant tables, which simulates retrieval noise. That is borderline. The benchmark is a resource, but it does include experiments. Still, it is not clearly a RAG system, since there is no retrieval step. The closest exclusion is E1 (not about RAG), though the robustness analysis against retrieved-like tables gives some basis for uncertainty, so MAYBE is a reasonable call.

The rule says to include only if the paper "runs experiments with a RAG system," and here the LLM is conditioned on given tables, not on retrieved documents. The pseudo-relevant tables setting resembles RAG, but the abstract does not make that clear. Because of this uncertainty, I choose MAYBE.

{"decision": "MAYBE", "code": null, "reason": "Table QA benchmark; RAG only touched via pseudo-relevant table robustness, unclear if RAG system."}
